  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={10}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/0fae83bb6962fff7c1b182e4ba2e548a95e4fbe5527e14eb6607b905129e503e.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $2$ units to the right to give $y={10}^{x-2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/bb9d5c110388591d1965117724fe683bb0af6e45f4e3b60c95bc959b14d76afe.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{10}^{x-2}.$ The point $(2,1)$ moves to $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943741/assets/5cae0046253d7ebc3c93000bad49d4d0e8b3653597fcbd2f16468c679ebe0116.png)",
